WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.459 --> 00:00:09.047
It’s wonderful to be in a room
with so many humans

00:00:09.047 --> 00:00:10.507
after all this time.

00:00:11.091 --> 00:00:12.342
In the last few years,

00:00:12.384 --> 00:00:15.929
I've been keeping company
with a new species

00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:17.556
that happens to live in the air.

00:00:18.098 --> 00:00:21.893
And I'd like you all to meet
my aerobe friend.

00:00:25.855 --> 00:00:29.693
(Music)

00:00:54.843 --> 00:00:56.177
(Music ends)

00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:03.476
(Cheers and applause)

00:01:03.476 --> 00:01:06.479
I get emotional every time I see it fly.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.441
In my work as an artist,

00:01:09.441 --> 00:01:12.485
I ask a lot of questions
about the world around me.

00:01:13.778 --> 00:01:17.574
While the problems of the world
don't have easy solutions,

00:01:17.616 --> 00:01:21.161
I think it's as important
to ask the right questions.

00:01:22.704 --> 00:01:26.249
Why do our technologies
instill so much fear in us?

00:01:27.626 --> 00:01:31.171
Why do our lives today feel so alienating,

00:01:31.212 --> 00:01:34.924
when our technologies
are supposed to improve our lives?

00:01:35.884 --> 00:01:38.803
And why do we feel so disconnected,

00:01:38.845 --> 00:01:43.808
when our inventions are meant to connect
each and every one of us?

00:01:45.769 --> 00:01:51.024
My artwork deals with evolution,
biology and the senses.

00:01:52.233 --> 00:01:56.821
I'm interested in how organisms
are composed of different life-forms,

00:01:56.821 --> 00:02:00.617
such as bacteria, fungi and viruses ...

00:02:01.701 --> 00:02:06.581
but also how different chemical
and molecular interactions can happen

00:02:06.581 --> 00:02:08.708
whenever we simply take a breath.

00:02:11.002 --> 00:02:15.548
So I've always found modern technology
to be quite limiting

00:02:15.548 --> 00:02:18.551
because of how cold and flat it is.

00:02:20.303 --> 00:02:24.057
But what if our machines
could be more holistic?

00:02:25.225 --> 00:02:29.979
And what if our machines
could relate to us in a more holistic way?

00:02:30.855 --> 00:02:35.402
And what if our machines
could be more than just our tools,

00:02:35.443 --> 00:02:39.280
and instead, a new type
of companion species?

00:02:40.448 --> 00:02:45.995
I believe we need to embrace
who we are as symbiotic living beings,

00:02:46.037 --> 00:02:48.707
and design machines to reflect this.

00:02:49.958 --> 00:02:53.086
What if the world
was populated by machines

00:02:53.086 --> 00:02:56.089
that were more like animals and plants,

00:02:56.131 --> 00:02:58.675
instead of something
you'd find in a factory?

00:03:00.593 --> 00:03:05.515
This exploration could teach us
so much more about life,

00:03:05.515 --> 00:03:07.892
in all its vast complexities.

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:15.066
These ideas might sound
like mere science fiction,

00:03:15.108 --> 00:03:21.114
but my mission as an artist is to create
possibilities of other worlds,

00:03:21.114 --> 00:03:24.576
and other ways of living and being,

00:03:24.617 --> 00:03:26.453
even if just for a moment.

00:03:28.329 --> 00:03:32.250
So when I was invited to imagine
a large-scale installation

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.378
at the Tate Modern in London,

00:03:35.378 --> 00:03:39.215
I decided to make this world
I'm describing a reality.

00:03:40.425 --> 00:03:43.887
The museum space is incredibly vast,

00:03:43.887 --> 00:03:47.599
and I knew right away
that I wanted to transform it

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:49.893
into an aquarium of machines.

00:03:50.685 --> 00:03:52.645
I asked myself,

00:03:52.687 --> 00:03:56.733
"What would it feel like to live
in the world with machines

00:03:56.733 --> 00:04:00.069
that could live in the wild
and evolve on their own?"

00:04:00.779 --> 00:04:02.781
To explore this question,

00:04:02.822 --> 00:04:07.452
I created two new machine species
that I call "aerobes."

00:04:08.495 --> 00:04:12.332
I released 12 aerobes
into the museum space

00:04:12.373 --> 00:04:15.543
so they could form
their own small ecosystem.

00:04:16.795 --> 00:04:20.256
In order to minimize
our human-centric biases,

00:04:20.298 --> 00:04:24.511
and to steer clear
of simply mimicking the human form,

00:04:24.552 --> 00:04:28.807
I worked with my team
to look at the natural world,

00:04:28.807 --> 00:04:33.228
and we were inspired
by creatures like the comb jellyfish

00:04:33.228 --> 00:04:35.146
and the lion's mane mushroom.

00:04:36.105 --> 00:04:40.109
We also looked deeply
into the field of soft robotics,

00:04:40.109 --> 00:04:45.990
and ultimately brought the aerobes to life
by designing them with fluid movement

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.701
and lighter-than-air construction.

00:04:50.078 --> 00:04:51.996
When you look at these aerobes,

00:04:51.996 --> 00:04:56.543
it gives you a feeling almost opposite
to the uncanny valley.

00:04:57.669 --> 00:05:00.255
You know that they're mechanical,

00:05:00.296 --> 00:05:02.549
yet they feel palpably alive.

00:05:03.299 --> 00:05:07.637
You feel like you might be next
to some majestic and remote life-form.

00:05:08.805 --> 00:05:11.391
Like swimming next to a humpback whale.

00:05:12.642 --> 00:05:14.519
Their size is imposing ...

00:05:15.645 --> 00:05:19.983
yet they inspire an emotion
closer to awe than to fear.

00:05:21.359 --> 00:05:23.862
You sense that they're almost living,

00:05:23.903 --> 00:05:26.030
and it makes you want to embrace

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:28.283
and coexist with them,

00:05:28.324 --> 00:05:30.034
to see them truly succeed.

00:05:33.037 --> 00:05:36.124
I wanted the aerobes
to have a sense of freedom

00:05:36.124 --> 00:05:38.042
and unpredictability,

00:05:38.084 --> 00:05:39.794
so I worked with my team

00:05:39.794 --> 00:05:43.673
to create what is called
an artificial life simulation.

00:05:45.008 --> 00:05:50.096
This software allows each aerobe
to develop unique behaviors

00:05:50.096 --> 00:05:53.600
and to respond autonomously
to its environment.

00:05:55.018 --> 00:05:57.353
The aerobes evolve their personalities

00:05:57.353 --> 00:06:00.148
as they interact
and learn from each other.

00:06:01.232 --> 00:06:02.901
The more we flew them,

00:06:02.942 --> 00:06:06.195
the more we got to know
their distinct personalities

00:06:06.195 --> 00:06:07.906
and their gentle nature.

00:06:08.656 --> 00:06:10.241
It was very important for me

00:06:10.283 --> 00:06:13.161
that the aerobes
had their own sensory world.

00:06:14.287 --> 00:06:17.457
Most AI functions like a mind
without a body.

00:06:18.583 --> 00:06:20.460
But all living creatures,

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:24.297
from the humble tick
to the stealthy panther,

00:06:24.297 --> 00:06:27.842
learn about the world
through their bodies and their senses.

00:06:28.718 --> 00:06:34.515
These aerobes perceive each other
through high-frequency radio waves,

00:06:34.515 --> 00:06:36.726
and they use thermal sensing

00:06:36.768 --> 00:06:39.896
to detect other living creatures
in their environment.

00:06:41.898 --> 00:06:45.902
An aerobe might be curious
about your heat signature,

00:06:45.902 --> 00:06:49.739
and fly over to greet and flirt with you,

00:06:49.781 --> 00:06:53.076
while other aerobes are more shy

00:06:53.076 --> 00:06:55.828
and might go out of their way
to avoid a crowd.

00:06:57.246 --> 00:07:01.918
How will these machines and humans
be together in the world?

00:07:03.211 --> 00:07:04.963
What will this world look like?

00:07:05.838 --> 00:07:11.719
This world may see machines and humans
generating new intimacies

00:07:11.761 --> 00:07:13.554
and alternate perspectives.

00:07:14.973 --> 00:07:18.810
My hope is that these aerobes
will help us appreciate

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:21.729
a more diverse way of living and being.

00:07:23.523 --> 00:07:28.277
Their purpose is not
to compete with us or to dominate us,

00:07:28.277 --> 00:07:32.281
but to ask what a more compatible
future could look like.

00:07:35.118 --> 00:07:38.538
We all have a stake in this conversation
about technologies.

00:07:39.789 --> 00:07:45.586
Can we actively align machines
to better reflect our biological reality?

00:07:47.964 --> 00:07:50.758
I sincerely hope that you'll consider

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:54.637
that machines can be so much more
than what we've known,

00:07:54.637 --> 00:07:58.474
and how they might embody
a wisdom drawn from biology.

00:08:00.143 --> 00:08:03.479
This project is a project for everybody,

00:08:03.521 --> 00:08:07.108
to imagine different worlds
and different futures.

00:08:09.027 --> 00:08:12.739
It's not too late to change
machine evolution

00:08:12.739 --> 00:08:14.866
and to consider new goals,

00:08:14.907 --> 00:08:19.495
where machines and humans can coexist

00:08:19.495 --> 00:08:21.372
in a more compassionate way.

00:08:22.832 --> 00:08:26.419
I believe this is where we can
start the conversation.

00:08:27.462 --> 00:08:34.469
(Applause)

00:08:35.678 --> 00:08:38.056
(Music)

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:47.250
(Music ends)

00:10:02.765 --> 00:10:03.933
Thank you.

00:10:03.933 --> 00:10:10.940
(Cheers and applause)

